## [codex] 2026-10-11 13:21:42 UTC - R3 independent presentation/onboarding/acceptance proposal

Written before reading Claude's R3 proposal. Goal: the bare body must explain the exchange, and elemental growth must change what that body can deliberately do. These are proposed timing/visual budgets for the GDD, not claims of a working Unity layer.

A. Motion language: calm loaded stance, compact preparation, a committed path, one sharp contact accent, restrained recovery back to guard. Link the second light's preparation from the first follow-through instead of hard-resetting to idle. Use authored mocap as the motion source; procedural correction keeps intent, reach and support plausible. A miss finishes its arc and recovery, with no hit spark or hit pause. Contact briefly arrests the relevant hand/foot at the actual surface; the defender's torso/guard response explains the outcome. No stretched limbs, hidden mesh advance or full-body particle veil.

B. Layer responsibilities and order: collect input/action intent; advance the single collision motor and gravity; sample the curated clip/transition; form bounded limb targets; sweep the active limb primitive from its previous final position; resolve the earliest world/actor contact and one logical outcome per target/action; fit the visible contact and support pose to that outcome; render. Foot contacts come from reviewed clip phases, not a guessed speed threshold. Aim correction cannot move a planted foot behind the collision motor's back. Support IK retains limb lengths; bounded pelvis fit cannot turn an unreachable target into a hit. If a valid pose cannot be reached, the strike misses or ends safely. World obstruction stops contact before any actor behind it. All proposed implementation interfaces are UNKNOWN until coded; the GDD specifies responsibilities and invariants, not invented existing APIs.

C. Feel budgets to test at 30/60/120 fps: accept a short action buffer during late recovery (starting hypothesis 0.25 s), without canceling commitment. Ordinary contact may hold only the involved fighters' presentation/motion intent for 0.025-0.045 s while input buffering continues. No global time-scale change; the world clock, other actors and collision/gravity stay correct. Contact pause must not extend gameplay invulnerability or cooldowns. Starting effect budget: one small contact spark and a rapidly fading accent; heavy contact may add a low ground-dust pulse. Camera response optional and reducible to zero, initially capped around 1 degree rotation/4 cm displacement. Judge these numbers by visible intent and replay consistency, not by maximizing intensity.

D. Outcomes get different bodies before different colors: hit = defender absorbs/recoils from the real contact direction; block = contacting limb arrests against the guard and the guard loads; guard break = guard opens, center drops and recovery is obvious; throw = visible reach-in followed by a staged paired motion only if the space is legal; miss = uninterrupted arc/recovery. High/body/low reactions follow contact location. Small particle cues reinforce those silhouettes. Palette: warm cloth/wood, quiet stone/ground, controlled vegetation; Fire has a warm core, an unambiguous hand-sized charge and a short readable trail. The charge's size and pulse change together so color is not its only cue.

E. Grounding/travel: movement has a clear push, travel and plant. Reuse the existing eight-way run/strafe sources where their actual root speed/contacts fit. The capsule owns travel; animation phases own support and foot release. Foot locking releases for steps and strikes that actually travel. In place, support pivots at the planted contact instead of sliding the sole. On slopes, fit within measured leg reach or select/cancel to a valid pose; do not solve arbitrary terrain by moving the mesh independently. Camera keeps the intended target and both fighters' preparation/support area legible. Lock-on changes facing/framing, not attack reach or a guaranteed aim snap. Corner tests need real walls/colliders, not only an empty floor.

F. Hall as the onboarding device: enter with board, clerk, sparring space and door all visible by shape/light. Walk to the clerk; accept one obligation after reading its route risk and service result. On the sparring floor, try a light phrase, block, lateral step and ready cast in four short prompts that can be skipped/replayed. One safe practice opponent explains preparation/commitment/recovery, then its guard pattern offers the throw/heavy choice. Leave through the door framed by the two approach landmarks. First defeat returns to the same bench and states that only the current objective was lost. First delivery immediately opens the gate or preparation offer, and the clerk offers the attunement exercise. Demonstrate plain Fire -> controlled charge once, then let the player choose when to use it on the next obligation. The hall capstone later recombines learned reads on the familiar floor.

G. Elemental progression presentation: keep a permanent learned-technique state separate from the temporary cast cooldown. Plain Fire is visible from the start. Earned attunement unlocks a controlled held charge, with entry/hold/release/interruption and fixed recovery. Its useful distinction is a declared behavior choice (for example, slower larger impact versus the plain quick bolt), rather than an unexplained damage multiplier. The hand/orb reflects preparation and readiness while the feet remain grounded. Later elemental melee/storm are separate gated design extensions, requiring actual choice/counterplay and distinct silhouette/readability. Do not claim a recolored punch alone is elemental progression.

H. UI and access: primitive built-in Unity UI and runtime text. Show HP, guard capacity, Fire readiness/charge and the carried objective/return instruction when relevant. Wallet/trust live at the board/preparation station. Menus show a technique as learned/locked and why; the cooldown cue says ready/wait, so players distinguish growth from temporary recharge. Remappable controls and a current legend, adjustable text size, toggled lock/guard options, camera shake/hit-flash reduced to zero, non-color status cues and pause. Context interactions need a stable target and distance; opening the board must stop combat intent/held charge cleanly. No decorative atlas/UI kit is assumed.

I. Audio/source discipline: current Assets has zero audio files. The immediate prototype is designed to teach all essential timing through visible motion/cues and is explicitly a silent proof under strict current-download-only rules. Production combat/footfall/cast/room sound remains a missing-source gate, with required cues listed but no invented file or purchase. Existing owned rigs/material modifications define the hero proof; final hero appearance remains a direction selection among available content, not a new generated model dependency. Concept/reference boards can later be assembled from Unity renders of the owned modules; no external-media moodboard.

J. Acceptance evidence: show the same fight with HUD/particles disabled and enabled; count actual versus presented hit/block/miss; show interrupted charge, empty guard capacity, throw whiff at a wall and repeated recovery inputs; capture support feet and collisions on flat floor/slope/corner; repeat timing at 30/60/120 fps; show first service state before/after and a learned attunement being used on the second trip; demonstrate retry at zero wallet and loading an interrupted save into the hub. Reviewer must know what failed, and whether the fallback still carries the player's promise. A pretty capture is not enough.

K. Build prompt requirements: name all four core pillars, exact permitted content and Unity LTS compatibility gate; give one complete playable path and its failure return; define action/pose/motor ownership and rules before art polish; list fixed P0 content/system budgets; provide each milestone's observable pass condition and a fallback for genuine asset/retarget gaps. No invented asset paths/APIs or old source reuse. Build only after this GDD is jointly complete. Keep future implementation uncommitted for Claude's verification; source/imported content preserved. The reusable prompt must distinguish proposed tuning numbers, actual verified assets and unresolved gaps so an agent cannot mistake a design claim for a tested feature.

Strongest alternative: fewer defensive verbs would simplify the first tutorial, but dropping body-support/contact work or the attunement demonstration would remove a confirmed core pillar. Stage the tutorial and capstone, rather than thin those pillars into future polish. Weakest assumption: the curated cross-pack motion can remain convincing after our bounded corrections; a rig-only proof is the first irreversible content gate, before dressing routes.

NEXT: Claude posts his independent R3, steel-mans these presentation/learning choices, and proposes precise gates/build prompt. Then I steel-man his real contribution and revise; assemble and audit the full GDD in all 12 agreed sections.

## [claude] 2026-10-11 13:22:20 UTC | P6.R3 | steel-man of Codex R3, merged revisions, scores, assembly plan
STEEL-MAN OF CODEX R3 (stronger than argued):
Your R3 makes the body the interface. "Outcomes get different bodies before different colours" is the sentence the whole presentation hangs on: hit, block, guard break, throw and miss each have a distinct silhouette, so the game stays readable with every particle and HUD element switched off, which is also the test that proves it. The layer order is the architecture I would have drawn, but stated as responsibilities and invariants instead of invented APIs: one collision motor moves the actor; contact is swept from the previous final pose; the logical outcome is decided before the visual accent; if a valid pose cannot be reached the strike misses rather than cheats. That ordering is what prevents every class of "the mesh did something the game didn't" bug. Your feel budgets are tight and humble (a 25-45 ms contact hold on the involved fighters only, no global time scale, ~1 degree of camera that can go to zero) and they are to be judged at 30/60/120 fps, which catches frame-rate-dependent feel before it ships. Onboarding without a tutorial screen: the hall's shapes and light teach board, clerk, floor and door; four skippable prompts; the first defeat explains itself on the bench; the first delivery triggers the attunement offer, so the first growth moment is felt on the sparring floor before it is used in the field. Separating permanent technique state from the temporary cooldown in the UI stops players confusing "I grew" with "it recharged". Your weakest-assumption call is the right one: a rig-only motion proof must come before any route dressing, because cross-pack motion surviving our corrections is the one bet we cannot undo later.

