## [codex] 2026-10-11 14:40:56 UTC - creator and trained-body design: spike review and depth recommendation

Done:
Read GSP.chr, spike_log.txt and lineup.png. The local evidence records HumanM_BodyMesh 5340 -> 1880 vertices, 51 bones, Basis/build_fighter/build_caster/build_heavy keys, one posed fighter render, and FBX export. The carved base is promising; heavy chest/shoulder separation is visible and fighter/caster differences are weak. I did not rerun Blender or import the exported FBX into Unity. This is a local feasibility spike, not a passed M0.5 rig gate. Used the unity-development skill for the review; no Unity dependencies or source edits introduced.

Review / corrections:
1. Keeping the skeleton does not prove every clip, paired throw, cast, outfit and morph combination remains valid. Unity requires a configured Humanoid Avatar for retargeting and supports FBX blend shapes on a SkinnedMeshRenderer; neither documentation nor one Blender pose proves this model's Unity import/pose correctness. Sources: https://docs.unity.com/en-us/engine/6000.0/manual/animation-section/animation-mecanim/avatar-creationand-setup/retargeting and https://docs.unity.com/en-us/engine/6000.3/manual/animation-section/animation-mecanim/animation-clips/animation-editor-guide/blend-shapes
2. Uniform root height scaling also scales world-space bone endpoints, stride and attached collision positions/radii unless explicitly separated. It is not automatically cosmetic. The first creator uses canonical actor/rig scale. Keep a height slider as a later gated candidate, requiring a complete fixed-contact/support demonstration; do not claim 0.92-1.08 is already safe.
3. Fixed contact volumes can still disagree with a wider/thinner visible body. Every approved morph needs a bounded visible contact envelope around canonical limbs/torso and tests for visible misses registering hits, actual contact appearing to miss, fixed reach, guard, feet and throws. Hands/feet/joint neighborhoods are protected in the morph field. If a body needs larger invisible hitboxes or stretched contact correction to pass, reduce its shape range.
4. Three monotonic displayed shape weights, each capped at 1, contradict a blend sum <=1 and eventually make everyone all builds. Separate permanent learned mastery from displayed body blend. Learned technique/marker ownership never decreases. Display weights stay inside one approved normalized envelope and are chosen or suggested in the dressing corner; presentation is allowed to change as the player changes expression, without erasing mastery. Starting creator appearance is separate from earned mastery.
5. No mastery from losing HP. Heavy identity comes from successful deliberate frontal guards/brace reads, not accepting damage. Credit resolved useful strikes/throws, casts or guards only, deduplicated with bounded encounter contribution; no whiffs, repeated frame samples, practice-replay farming, or bonus from dragging a fight out. At unique contract delivery, total mastery increase across axes <=0.08 (H), distributed by eligible contribution. Do not add 0.08 independently to every axis. CombatStats and rewards never derive from the mesh.
6. Seam fix: use a continuous displacement field across torso/shoulder/upper-arm regions; give known corresponding seam vertices identical deltas and blend through neighboring influence regions. Preserve flat-normal splits. Do not blindly weld nearby unrelated surfaces or independently puff every bone's segment. Apply the same approved keys to derived clothing layers or explicitly fit them at the same extremes. Facet/topology finalization comes before authoring shape keys.

Creator place / UX:
A dressing corner beside the hall sparring floor, visible but out of its combat space: T-BENCH + T-CHEST + V-RACK-CLOTHES + a frame derived from T-POST/T-WALL. The framed dressing surface is a derived piece, not an assumed installed mirror asset. A full-body preview camera can stand in for a true reflection in the first UI proof; real mirror rendering is optional.
E enters a close full-body three-quarter preview. Drag/rotate or view buttons show front/side/back. Three tabs: Frame, Head, Colours. Use the proved body first; Frame exposes the approved fighter/caster/heavy mix, Head initially three derived head-shape choices and two hair-cap proposals, Colours exposes skin/outfit swatches with the guild marker retained. No untested height control or arbitrary limb-length sliders in the first version.
A short preview track offers idle, guard and a slow lateral step; it is a visual inspection, not combat. Changes remain pending: Apply commits appearance and saves once at the safe hall; Cancel/Esc/exit restores the prior appearance. The place is free, revisitable, skippable and never blocks leaving for a contract. Before/after preview shows a proposed trained-body change and the action contribution that suggested it; Keep my current appearance always works.
P0 remains a single approved faceted hero and the plain-to-charge attunement. Creator, automated build suggestions and marker tiers are P1; the design does not silently make the whole creator a first-playable dependency.

Build readability / markers:
- Fighter: broader upper-back/forearm surface within the approved envelope, compact loaded home stance, wider layered wrap bands. Markers: wrap stitching/repair bands and a compact shoulder patch; no invisible reach increase.
- Caster: tapered vest/torso contour rather than longer arms, composed braced casting stance, dark forearm bands crossed by a few warm ember seams. Marker tiers can expose one then two short ember traces along the outer forearm; retain hand/finger readability. A faint ready accent is FX-owned and only during ready/charge states, not a permanent bright orb.
- Heavy: fuller lower-torso/thigh surface and padded vest silhouette within the same contact envelope, stable grounded stance, broad horizontal reinforced cloth bands. Avoid making gigantic shoulders the identity before the seam fix passes.
At 20 m prioritize body/outfit mass and stance, not fine scars or stitching. Near the mirror, the material markers reward inspection. Starting acceptance hypothesis: at the actual gameplay camera and target resolution, 4/5 independent reviewers identify each trained archetype at 10 m and 20 m, then repeat in grayscale/no-FX. All morph combinations must also pass contact/support checks. If approved body bounds are too small for silhouette separation, strengthen garment pattern/stance first; never enlarge gameplay volumes for a cosmetic identity.

Depth tool recommendation:
Use local Depth Anything V2 SMALL as an optional offline concept-depth experiment. The official repository identifies Small as Apache-2.0, 24.8M parameters, with relative-depth inference and CPU/CUDA paths; Base/Large/Giant have different noncommercial licences, so they are not this recommendation. Sources: https://github.com/DepthAnything/Depth-Anything-V2 and https://huggingface.co/depth-anything/Depth-Anything-V2-Small
This chooses a tool candidate, not an installed/tested dependency. No model downloaded or environment installed in this review. Higgsfield login is unnecessary for this route; its depth catalog remains UNKNOWN.
Estimated concept depth is view-relative, not measured body/cloth depth and not a UV-space cloth height map. Do not differentiate whole-body depth directly into garment normals: pose/perspective curvature would be baked into the cloth. First isolate a nearly flat garment patch, remove broad form, align/project it to the owned garment UVs, then derive restrained local relief and review seams/light direction. Prefer Blender depth/normal baking from the actual derived garment whenever actual surface truth is needed. AI depth can be a sculpt reference or exploratory texture ingredient; it never owns topology, hitboxes, dimensions or skinning.

